PHP Code Structure:
```php
<?php
// Input reading
$input = [];
$stdin = fopen('php://stdin', 'r');
while (($line = fgets($stdin)) !== false) {
    $input[] = rtrim($line, "\n\r");
}
fclose($stdin);

// Iterator object
$obj = [
    'list' => $input,
    'index' => 0,
    'max' => count($input),
    'hasNext' => function() use (&$obj) { return $obj['index'] < $obj['max']; },
    'next' => function() use (&$obj) { 
        if ($obj['hasNext']()) { 
            return $obj['list'][$obj['index']++]; 
        } else { 
            throw new Exception('ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException'); 
        } 
    }
];

// Helper functions
function next() { global $obj; return $obj['next'](); }
function hasNext() { global $obj; return $obj['hasNext'](); }
function nextInt() { return intval(next()); }
function myout($s) { echo $s . "\n"; }
function myconv($i, $no) {
    try {
        switch($no) {
            case 1: return intval($i);
            case 2: return explode(' ', $i);
            case 4: return array_map('intval', explode(' ', $i));
            case 6: return str_split($i);
            case 7: return array_map('intval', str_split($i));
            case 8: return implode(' ', $i);
            case 9: return implode("\n", $i);
            case 0: return implode('', $i);
            default: return $i;
        }
    } catch (Exception $e) {
        return $i;
    }
}

function Main() {
    $N = nextInt(); // Read N
    $line = next(); // Read the string line
    $s = explode("W", $line);
    $output = [];
    foreach ($s as $segment) {
        if ($segment !== "") {
            $output[] = strlen($segment);
        }
    }
    myout(count($output));
    myout(myconv($output, 8));
}

Main();
?>
```

Check Node.js `next()` usage in Main:
`var N = nextInt();` -> reads first line as int.
`var s = next().split("W");` -> reads second line, splits by "W".

The PHP code above does exactly that.

One detail: Node's `readline` splits by newline. `fgets` keeps newline, so `rtrim` is needed.
Also, `init` in JS creates an object with methods. In PHP, using an array with closures capturing by reference (`use (&$obj)`) works for the iterator state.

The `myconv` function in JS handles arrays and strings. In PHP, `implode` expects array. The JS code passes `output` (array) to `myconv(output, 8)`. `output` contains integers. `implode(' ', $output)` works fine in PHP (integers cast to string).

The JS `myconv` switch case 8: `return i.join(' ');`.
PHP `implode(' ', $i)`.

The JS code has `var s = next().split("W");`. If the input string contains spaces? The problem seems to be "B - W" from AtCoder or similar (counting consecutive non-W characters). Usually the string is a single line without spaces. `next()` reads a full line.

The Node.js boilerplate reads all lines into `inLine`. `init` makes an iterator. `next()` returns the next line.
So `nextInt()` reads line 1. `next()` reads line 2.

The PHP translation looks solid.

